NAME
       systemd-cat - send text or a program's output to the journal

SYNOPSIS
       systemd-cat [-t IDENTIFIER] [-p PRIORITY] [COMMAND...]

DESCRIPTION
       systemd-cat writes into the systemd journal. Without a command it
       stores each line it reads on standard input as a journal message; with
       a command it runs it and stores the command's output. Scripts use it so
       their messages can be searched with journalctl.

OPTIONS
       -t, --identifier=NAME
              The name the messages are filed under (journalctl -t NAME).

       -p, --priority=LEVEL
              The priority: emerg, alert, crit, err, warning, notice, info
              (default) or debug.

EXAMPLES
           echo "backup finished" | systemd-cat -t backup
           journalctl -t backup

SEE ALSO
       journalctl(1), logger(1)
